      <h1 class="font-serif-sc text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight text-stone-900 mt-3">
        Chinese Clothing Across the Dynasties
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-stone-500 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Every Chinese dynasty had a distinctive fashion identity, and remarkably, you can still visit the cities that shaped them. Walk the Silk Road starting point in Xi''an (Han dynasty), explore Song dynasty elegance in Hangzhou, and trace Ming imperial style through Beijing and Nanjing.
      </p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Han Dynasty (206 BC–220 AD)</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The Han dynasty gave us the very word ''Hanfu'' (\u6f22\u670d) — clothing of the Han people. The classic Han silhouette featured a wrapped outer robe (jiaoling youren, meaning crossed collar worn right-over-left) with deep overlapping lapels and wide, flowing sleeves that could extend past the fingertips. A sash (dadai) cinched the waist, often with jade ornaments hanging from the belt. Silk was the prestige fabric, with sericulture flourishing along the Silk Road''s eastern terminus in Chang''an (modern Xi''an). Commoners wore hemp and ramie. Color hierarchy was strict: officials wore specific colors and patterns based on their rank, while the emperor alone could wear certain shades of yellow.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mt-3"><strong>Best place to experience:</strong> Xi''an — walk the Silk Road starting point, visit the Shaanxi History Museum for Han dynasty textile remnants.</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Tang Dynasty (618–907 AD)</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The Tang era was China''s most cosmopolitan period — and its most daring in fashion. Women wore high-waisted ruqun skirts cinched above the chest, paired with short, open-front jackets (banbi) that exposed the neck and collarbone. Bold colors ruled: crimson, imperial yellow, emerald green. Central Asian and Persian influences arrived via the Silk Road, introducing woven gold brocades, Sassanian roundel motifs, and even trousers for noble women who rode horses. The huadian — a decorative painted or pasted flower on the forehead — became the signature Tang beauty mark. Men''s fashion featured round-collared robes with narrow sleeves and leather boots, reflecting the dynasty''s martial spirit. The Tang court issued detailed sumptuary laws governing which colors, fabrics, and ornamentation each social rank could wear.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mt-3"><strong>Best places to experience:</strong> Xi''an (Daming Palace ruins host Tang costume festivals), Dunhuang (Mogao Caves murals vividly depict Tang fashion in Buddhist art).</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Song Dynasty (960–1279 AD)</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Where Tang was bold, Song was restrained. Neo-Confucian philosophy emphasized modesty and simplicity, producing a fashion of refined understatement that still reads as modern today. Women''s garments narrowed: sleeves became fitted at the wrist, silhouettes grew straighter and less voluminous. The beizi — a long, sleeveless or narrow-sleeved outer jacket worn open at the front — became the dynasty''s signature piece, often reaching the ankles. Color palettes shifted to muted tones: celadon green, misty blue, pale lavender, ash gray. The literati scholar-official class drove men''s fashion with simple, wide robes (daopao) and the iconic square-topped hat (putou). Embroidery became more refined as a domestic art, with Song embroiderers achieving unprecedented levels of detail on silk.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mt-3"><strong>Best place to experience:</strong> Hangzhou — the Southern Song imperial capital, where you can wear Song-style Hanfu by West Lake and visit the China National Silk Museum.</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Ming Dynasty (1368–1644 AD)</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">After nearly a century of Mongol Yuan rule, the Ming dynasty explicitly revived Han Chinese clothing traditions as an act of cultural restoration. The iconic mamianqun (horse-face skirt) emerged: a wrap-around pleated skirt with flat front and back panels, typically in bright red or blue brocade. Women paired these with stand-collar jackets (ao) that closed with ornate metal or jade buttons. The Ming introduced the buzi system — square embroidered rank badges sewn onto the chest and back of officials'' robes, with birds denoting civil ranks and animals for military ranks. The circular collar (yuanlingpao) with coordinating cross-collar inner robe became the standard formal silhouette. Cloud-collar (yunjian) shawls with four cloud-shaped lobes were worn as decorative overlays. Ming fashion is the primary reference for today''s Hanfu revival movement.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mt-3"><strong>Best places to experience:</strong> Beijing (Forbidden City and Ming Tombs), Nanjing (Ming Xiaoling Mausoleum — stunning red-wall photography backdrop).</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Qing Dynasty (1644–1912)</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The Manchu conquest brought dramatic changes. The qizhuang (banner dress) introduced the changyi — a long, straight robe with side slits and wide sleeves with folded cuffs — which directly influenced the modern qipao. Manchu women wore a distinctive three-earring style and platform shoes with elevated central heels. Han men were required to adopt the Manchu queue hairstyle (shaved forehead with a long braid), though Han women largely retained Ming-style dress for informal occasions. Court dress reached its apex of elaboration: officials wore layered dragon robes (jifu for semi-formal, chaofu for the most ceremonial occasions) with intricate rank badges, and empresses and high-ranking consorts wore phoenix coronets adorned with kingfisher feather inlay. The Qing textile industry produced some of history''s most elaborate silk embroidery.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mt-3"><strong>Best places to experience:</strong> Shenyang (Mukden Palace, the original Manchu imperial seat), Beijing (Forbidden City Qing collections), Shanghai (where the Qing changyi evolved into the 1920s qipao).</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Republican Era (1912–1949)</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The fall of the Qing in 1912 triggered China''s most rapid fashion transformation. The 1920s Shanghai qipao (cheongsam) fused Manchu robe structure with Western tailoring: body-hugging darts, higher side slits, and luxurious imported fabrics. This was China''s first genuinely modern women''s garment, championed by socialites, movie stars, and the "modern girl" (modeng nvlang) who defined Shanghai''s jazz-age glamour. The 1930s qipao reached its peak — floor-length with high mandarin collars and delicate frog-button closures, often in printed silk. Simultaneously, the Zhongshan suit (based on Sun Yat-sen''s design combining Western tailoring with Chinese symbolism) became the standard men''s formalwear. The era also saw the rise of the first Chinese fashion magazines, department stores, and domestic designers.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mt-3"><strong>Best places to experience:</strong> Shanghai — the Bund''s heritage architecture and the French Concession still echo this era. Visit the Shanghai History Museum for original 1930s qipaos.</p>
